A DEBUG INSTRUMENT, NOT A SERIES PATCH. Do not put it in an image.

What it is for. A caller times one interval around a submit -- the ioctl, then a wait
on the job's fence -- and on a graph that runs as one hardware kick that interval is
61-81% of the wall. From userspace it is opaque: there is no way to tell the part
running programs from the driver taking its time getting to them, and a performance
claim against the biggest term in a profile needs to know which it is.

This adds five microsecond accumulators to the RK3576 polled completion path, exposed
as writable module parameters, splitting that interval at the events only the driver
can see: the scheduler wakeup, the register programming, the kick's own execution, the
writing block's drain, and the fence signal. A sixth (`stat_total_us`, push -> fence
signal) is what says the five account for the caller's own number.

Applying it. The out-of-tree directories on the board are FLAT, so `patch -p4`, not -p1:

    cp -a ~/rocketoot_range ~/rocketoot_dbg && cd ~/rocketoot_dbg
    rm -f *.o *.ko *.mod *.mod.c modules.order
    patch -p4 --forward < rk3576-job-timing-debug.patch
    make -C ~/ksrc/linux-<ver> M=$PWD modules

The accumulators are `unsigned int`, not `unsigned long long`, deliberately: a rebuild
that imports a symbol the shipped module never did (`param_ops_ullong`) fails modpost
with "undefined!" for a symbol the kernel plainly exports, and working around that
means harvesting a CRC with tools/mksymvers.py. `uint` holds 71 minutes of accumulated
job time, which is far more than any one measurement arm.

Reading it.

    P=/sys/module/rocket/parameters
    for s in jobs tasks wakeup_us prologue_us exec_us drain_us signal_us total_us drain0; do
        echo 0 | sudo tee $P/stat_$s >/dev/null; done
    ... run the arm ...
    for s in ...; do printf '%s=%s ' $s "$(cat $P/stat_$s)"; done

A gate binary runs setup jobs before its bench loop, so `jobs` exceeds the iteration
count and a plain average is contaminated by them. Take bench(2N) minus bench(N): the
fixed setup cancels and the difference is exactly N clean inferences.

Two things the numbers mean less than they look like. `exec` is quantised UP by the
poll period (default 50 us), because it ends at the tick that OBSERVES the kick over,
so it is an upper bound on the part's own time by up to one period -- confirm with an
A/B on `poll_interval_us`. And `drain` is only the part of the writing block's drain
that had not already finished when that tick landed: on a one-kick graph it reads near
zero, and `drain0` counting every job is what says the completion bit was already up.

